    {
      "heading": "The family",
      "body": "*La familia* is central in Latin American life, and you'll talk about it a lot. The masculine plural covers mixed groups: *los padres* = parents (father + mother), *los hermanos* = brothers and sisters, *los hijos* = children (sons and daughters), *los abuelos* = grandparents.\n\nIn everyday speech, parents are usually *mamá* and *papá*; *la madre* and *el padre* are a bit more formal."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Possessive words: my, your, his, her, our, their",
      "body": "These go before the noun. *Mi, tu, su* only change for plural; *nuestro* changes for gender and number.",
      "table": {
        "head": ["", "Singular noun", "Plural noun"],
        "rows": [
          ["my", "*mi hermano*", "*mis hermanos*"],
          ["your (tú)", "*tu madre*", "*tus padres*"],
          ["his / her / its / your (usted)", "*su casa*", "*sus casas*"],
          ["our", "*nuestro padre* · *nuestra madre*", "*nuestros hijos* · *nuestras hijas*"],
          ["your (ustedes) / their", "*su familia*", "*sus abuelos*"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Watch out",
      "body": "- *Tu* (your) has no accent; *tú* (you) does: *Tú eres mi amigo y tu casa es bonita.*\n- The possessive agrees with the thing owned, not the owner: *nuestra casa* (our house) — *casa* is feminine.\n- *Su* can mean his, her, their, or your (formal). Context usually makes it clear; if not, say *la casa de Juan* (Juan's house). Spanish has no \"'s\": use *de* — *el perro de mi hermana* (my sister's dog)."
    }
  ],
